title: 6. Jeff is asked only what is to be built and what cannot be undone; the rest the team settles, and records so that he can reverse it.
pid: bf96bac6-0731-4694-9cb0-103a25be6b3b
body: |2-
  That is Tay's rule, [[id:97eeb762-3b30-432a-ac67-ec052df492b7][Ask about what is to be built, and what is hard to reverse. Keep going on how.]]
  A one-way door goes to Jeff even when Tay is sure of the answer. A
  two-way door does not, even when the author is unsure: decide on
  most of what you wish you knew, and write the decision down where
  he can find it and turn it around.
  Also to Jeff: a disagreement between Tay and the author that is
  still there after the readings are folded in. Raise it at once;
  do not let it be settled by whoever writes last.
  Top puts the questions in its 'to Jeff' together, one per headline,
  each with the recommended answer and the default. Missions that do
  not depend on an answer go ahead without waiting for it.
  Rests on: read (Bezos 2015: Type 1 and Type 2 decisions, and the
  warning that big organizations use the heavy process for both;
  Bezos 2016: decide on about 70% of the information, disagree and
  commit, escalate true misalignment early).
